For every test case we are given two rectangles.
For each rectangle the order of the two numbers is unknown, i.e. we may
swap its sides.

The original figure was a square.
A single horizontal or vertical cut was made, therefore the two
rectangles have

* a common side equal to the side of the square `S`,
* the other sides placed side by side fill the whole square,
  therefore they sum to `S`.

So we have to check whether we can choose an orientation of the two
rectangles such that one of the following holds  

```
h1 == h2 == S   and   w1 + w2 == S          (common height)
w1 == w2 == S   and   h1 + h2 == S          (common width)
```

`(w1 , h1)` and `(w2 , h2)` are the chosen orientations.

The numbers of possibilities are tiny – each rectangle has two
orientations, altogether `2·2 = 4` possibilities.
We simply try them all.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Algorithm
For each test case  

```
read a1, b1, a2, b2
for (x1 , y1) in [(a1,b1), (b1,a1)]:
    for (x2 , y2) in [(a2,b2), (b2,a2)]:
        if y1 == y2 and x1 + x2 == y1:   answer = YES
        if x1 == x2 and y1 + y2 == x1:   answer = YES
if none of the four checks succeeded → answer = NO
output answer
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm outputs “YES” **iff** the two rectangles can
originate from a square.

---

##### Lemma 1  
If the algorithm outputs “YES”, the two rectangles can be placed together
to form a square.

**Proof.**  
The algorithm prints “YES” only when for some orientations
`(w1,h1)` and `(w2,h2)` one of the two conditions holds.

*Case 1:* `h1 == h2 == S` and `w1 + w2 == S`.  
Both rectangles have height `S`, the side length of the square.
Placing them side‑by‑side horizontally their total width is `S`,
so together they exactly fill an `S × S` square.

*Case 2:* `w1 == w2 == S` and `h1 + h2 == S`.  
Symmetrically they have equal width `S` and together their heights sum to
`S`. Stacking them vertically also produces an `S × S` square. ∎



##### Lemma 2  
If the two rectangles can be placed together to form a square,
the algorithm will output “YES”.

**Proof.**  
Assume a square of side `S` can be obtained.
The cut is either horizontal or vertical.

*Horizontal cut.*  
Both resulting rectangles have height `S`. Their widths `x1 , x2`
satisfy `x1 + x2 = S`.  
The input gives each rectangle as an unordered pair of its sides,
so among the two orientations of the first rectangle one has height `S`,
the same for the second rectangle. Consequently the algorithm examines
exactly this pair of orientations, sees `h1 = h2 = S` and `w1 + w2 = S`,
and prints “YES”.

*Vertical cut* is completely symmetric and leads to the second
condition of the algorithm. ∎



##### Theorem  
For every test case the algorithm prints “YES” exactly when the given
rectangles could have been obtained by cutting a square with one
horizontal or vertical cut.

**Proof.**  
*If* part: Lemma&nbsp;1.  
*Only‑if* part: Lemma&nbsp;2. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Complexity Analysis  

For each test case we try at most `4` orientation pairs, each check is
`O(1)`.  
Time   : `O(t)`  
Memory : `O(1)` besides the input.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

